#4471 is not one bug. Its reports describe three problems, and each problem now has its own
ticket. The [#4471 page](/kb/incidents/4471) says "one issue, one fix queued". That fix covers
only the first problem below.

<ProblemTable
  ticket={4471}
  repo="GidianB/support-kb"
  problems={[
    { key: 'parser', what: 'The parser reads dates month first', issue: 1 },
    { key: 'hint', what: 'The booking-v3 hint says MM/DD/YYYY', issue: 2 },
    { key: 'one-day', what: 'Invites move by one day', issue: 3 },
  ]}
/>

## How the reports were split

1. Count each report once. The export repeats some rows. One id is one report.
2. Place each report by its tenant, not by its intake tag. The tenant's locale and booking form
   decide the cause (`causeOf`). Intake guesses the tag from the reporter's browser, and the
   guess is often wrong.
3. Take out the one-day moves the desk noted (`desk-notes.json`). In these reports the date
   moves by one day. Day and month do not swap.

## The parser reads dates month first

The shared parser reads every date month first. Our tenants write the day first, so day and
month swap: a booking for 7 June goes out for 6 July. The queued fix for #4471 is for this
problem. Every day-first tenant has it, through every channel.

## The booking-v3 hint says MM/DD/YYYY

The booking-v3 form prints MM/DD/YYYY under its date field. People at day-first tenants on
booking-v3 type the month first, as the hint tells them. The form then reads the date the
tenant's way, day first, and the date swaps. This happens only through the form. A fix to the
parser does not change it: the hint must show the tenant's own date order.

## Invites move by one day

These reports have no swap. The date is one day early or late:

- "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday. Same meeting. We held it twice."
- "The invite arrived before I made the booking."
- "Tuesday 6 October showed as Monday."

People in different places see different days for the same booking, so look at time zone
handling, not at date order. A fix to the parser does not change it.

<Callout kind="warn">

The three problems share subject lines such as "Wrong day on the invite". The desk merges on
subject alone ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)), so all three went into
one ticket. Before you merge a date report, check its tenant, its channel, and whether the day
moved by one.

</Callout>

